        "link": "https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/hong-kong-economy/article/3353009/100-hong-kong-linked-ships-stranded-strait-hormuz-amid-middle-east-war?utm_source=rss_feed",
        "orario": "15:30",
        "timestamp": 1778333436,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement Hong Kong economy Hong Kong Hong Kong Economy 100 Hong Kong-linked ships ‘stranded in Strait of Hormuz’ amid Middle East war Industry leader says figure suggests 2,300 seafarers trapped in strait, with sector’s biggest priority being keeping them supplied 3 -MIN READ 3 -MIN Lam Ka-sing Published: 9:30pm, 9 May 2026 About 100 ships that are either registered in Hong Kong or locally managed or owned are stranded in the strategic Strait of Hormuz amid the United States and Israel’s war with Iran, accounting for an estimated 2,300 trapped seafarers, an industry leader has said. Richard Hext, chairman of the Hong Kong Shipowners Association, told the South China Morning Post on Friday that it was very risky to sail vessels through the strait due to the risk of attack, even though the US and Iran had recently reached a temporary ceasefire. The war has sparked a global fuel crisis and disrupted supply chains as the narrow waterway handles 20 per cent of the world’s oil. Advertisement “If you have a ship that is stuck inside the Strait of Hormuz, it’s very risky to try and sail it out, because there’s a chance that someone will fire at the ship,” Hext said. He said the Hong Kong-linked ships stuck in the waterway were either registered in the city or managed or owned by Hong Kong companies. Advertisement “So if it’s 100 ships, and you calculate about 23 seafarers on each ship, then that is 2,300 seafarers who are stuck on the Hong Kong ships,” he added. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
